# Production Environment Variables

# NO API URL HERE, DELIBERATELY.
#
# The backend is reached SAME-ORIGIN in production: the app calls `/api/...` on
# whatever host serves it, and CloudFront routes `/api/*` to API Gateway. So a
# production build needs no backend configuration at all, and switching backends
# is a CloudFront change rather than a rebuild.
#
# This file used to set NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL to the Railway URL, which is how a
# CloudFront deploy went out silently talking to Railway while looking, from the
# outside, exactly like a build pointing at AWS. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are baked
# into the JavaScript at build time, so there is no way to tell which backend a
# deployed bundle chose without grepping the compiled chunks.
#
# Setting it here again re-creates that bug. If you genuinely need a build
# pointed at a specific backend, pass it on the command line for that one build:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://... npm run build
#
# See lib/api/base-url.ts, which is now the only place the backend URL is decided.

# Google Maps API Key.
# Public by construction — NEXT_PUBLIC_* ships in the browser bundle, so this is
# readable by anyone using the site. That is normal for Maps keys, but it means
# the protection has to be an HTTP-referrer restriction in the Google Cloud
# console, not secrecy. Worth confirming that restriction is actually set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=AIzaSyAH7PTzTVJrud5KqsDmWEw67mQkiA0Co4Y
